				<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default">
					<Content> 117, nos. 3–4 (2021), DOI: https://doi.org/10.17159/sajs.2021/9490; A. Gooden and D. W. Thaldar, ‘Despite Good Progress with Regard to the Proposed Code of Conduct for Research in South Africa, Unresolved Issues Remain’, </Content>
				</CharacterStyleRange>
				<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Italic">
					<Content>Humanities and Social Sciences Communications</Content>
				</CharacterStyleRange>
				<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default">
					<Content> (2024), DOI: https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-024-02715-0.﻿</Content>
				</CharacterStyleRange>
			</EndnoteRange>
			<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default">
				<Br />
				<EndnoteRange Self="uc1f" SourceEndnote="uc1e">
					<Content>﻿<?ACE 4?>	See, for example, clause 4.2.2.3 of the Code of Conduct, which states that researchers must ‘ensure that the Personal Information is Pseudonymised unless there is a compelling reason why it is not feasible or appropriate’.﻿</Content>
				</EndnoteRange>
				<Br />
